/target
# Cargo.lock IS checked in, deliberately. Cargo's own guidance is: commit it
# for anything that produces a binary, ignore it for a library. This workspace
# ships `patala-sidecar`, a binary — and one whose whole reason to exist is
# key isolation for a payment substrate. A clean clone that resolves its own
# dependency versions is not the build anyone tested; for this crate that
# means the exact bytes handling a signing key could differ between a
# maintainer's machine and a deployment. Reproducibility wins over the
# library-side argument (that a lockfile hides downstream version-range
# breakage), which `cargo update` in CI would be the right answer to anyway.
**/*.rs.bk
.claude/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
dist/
*.so
*.dylib
# UniFFI-generated bindings + the cdylib copied next to them are build output,
# reproduced by the documented `uniffi-bindgen` commands — not checked in
# (same reasoning as `/target`).
/patala-py/bindings/
/patala-uniffi/bindings/

# BEAM crash dumps — an Elixir NIF fault drops one of these at the repo root.
erl_crash.dump

# Where scripts/release-stage.sh stages published assets (and where the
# release workflow emits SHA256SUMS over). Build output: a checked-in copy of
# a release bundle would be a second set of bytes claiming to be the release.
/release/
